WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.559
<v Lina>"I have merely been transplanted from one scene of student-life to another." That's a young man writing from Cambridge in 1859.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:14.406
<v Maya>Transplanted. That's such a brave word for, "I already miss home."

00:00:14.406 --> 00:00:22.886
<v Theo>And we have that sentence because the letter became a book, the book became a scan, and the scan became crawler food.

00:00:22.886 --> 00:00:24.806
<v Maya>Crawler food?

00:00:24.806 --> 00:00:30.806
<v Theo>That's the technical term. Pen to pixel in one hundred and fifty-four years.

00:00:30.806 --> 00:00:41.365
<v Lina>And after all that survival, what survives emotionally is so simple: he knows home is about to become somewhere he visits.

00:00:41.365 --> 00:00:48.649
<v Maya>the idea of going away for good and all, and always hereafter being at home only as a visitor.

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:52.332
<v Theo>That phrase gets worse the longer you sit with it.

00:00:52.332 --> 00:00:56.812
<v Maya>Welcome to The Homes We Leave Behind. I'm Maya.

00:00:56.812 --> 00:00:57.452
<v Theo>I'm Theo.

00:00:57.452 --> 00:00:58.651
<v Lina>And I'm Lina.

00:00:58.651 --> 00:01:06.331
<v Theo>Quick note on where all this comes from: every source today was grabbed by a web crawler in December 2013.

00:01:06.331 --> 00:01:12.012
<v Lina>That's when it was captured, not necessarily when it was written. That 1859 letter?

00:01:12.012 --> 00:01:22.092
<v Maya>It was sitting on the Internet Archive as a raw text file—an OCR dump of a scanned book, complete with broken line breaks and a stray page number header.

00:01:22.092 --> 00:01:26.172
<v Lina>So December is the shelf date, not the birth date.

00:01:26.172 --> 00:01:32.253
<v Maya>Today we're asking what happens when the place you call home becomes a place you can only visit.

00:01:32.253 --> 00:01:40.332
<v Theo>And that ache turned up everywhere in this archive—old letters, blog posts, a grief journal on a health website.

00:01:40.332 --> 00:01:50.012
<v Lina>A century and a half later, a blogger chooses the name Saudade—a word for longing that never quite resolves.

00:01:50.012 --> 00:01:53.213
<v Maya>Wait. They named the blog after the feeling?

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.920
<v Lina>Exactly. They had left Cape Verde, and years later they still thought about it every day.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:07.519
<v Theo>So one writer calls leaving a transplant. Another names the whole archive after the ache.

00:02:07.519 --> 00:02:16.640
<v Theo>There never was a time when I was more myself, more comfortable with the skin I wore, the people I'd seen, the place I'd visited.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:24.160
<v Lina>What gets me is that Saudade's home isn't the place they grew up. It's a place they chose and then had to leave.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.479
<v Maya>And that kind of homesickness—try explaining that to someone.

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:34.320
<v Theo>The blog itself is a little mystery. The archive shows exactly one post.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:46.079
<v Lina>It's called "Raskalnikov"—yes, Dostoevsky—but the display name is Saudade. Two literary personas are sharing one page.

00:02:46.079 --> 00:02:50.079
<v Maya>And the Cape Verdean Creole isn't translated for outsiders?

00:02:50.079 --> 00:02:54.800
<v Theo>No. That part of the page stays with the people who already understand it.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.360
<v Maya>Maybe they weren't writing for us.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:04.081
<v Theo>Maybe they were writing for the friends they name at the end of the post—people still in Cape Verde who'd understand every word.

00:03:04.081 --> 00:03:08.480
<v Lina>Okay, now here's where Theo found something that floored me.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.800
<v Maya>In another OCR scan?

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:16.881
<v Lina>Another scan, page numbers and all. And this one is a letter from Beethoven.

00:03:16.881 --> 00:03:19.360
<v Maya>Wait. Beethoven?

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:30.321
<v Theo>It's addressed to Stephan von Breuning. Beethoven is writing about how his deafness keeps him from traveling—he can't go see the scenery of his fatherland, he can't marry.

00:03:30.321 --> 00:03:32.801
<v Lina>And then there's this line—

00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:41.760
<v Lina>Oh ! I could span the world were I only free from this ! I feel that my youth is only now commencing.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:50.001
<v Maya>He's homesick for a life he can't get to. Not a specific house—a whole future his deafness has walled off.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:04:02.960
<v Theo>And the digital version of the letter just cuts off mid-sentence. His closing words, his signature—gone. The scan or the extraction stopped before the end.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.881
<v Lina>So even this archived copy of a published copy of a private letter is incomplete.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:15.921
<v Lina>The silence belongs to the file, not necessarily to him.

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:25.760
<v Lina>Then there's Brooke Davidoff. And her story brings all of this into the present tense. She wrote a post called "Homesick" after her father died.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.801
<v Maya>She's living far from southern California, where her mother and her father's house are.

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:38.001
<v Theo>The family owns the house in Wildomar together, but nobody can afford the mortgage, and nobody lives nearby.

00:04:38.001 --> 00:04:45.921
<v Lina>I want to go home. The California heat and my mommy are calling. I'm not homesick, I'm heart sick.

00:04:45.921 --> 00:04:54.081
<v Theo>Her post cuts off mid-sentence too—she starts describing a dream at a party and the text just stops.

00:04:54.081 --> 00:04:58.641
<v Lina>We don't know if that's how she left it or if the crawl missed the rest.

00:04:58.641 --> 00:05:04.720
<v Lina>Twice now, the archive has closed the door before the ending.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:15.361
<v Maya>Four voices across two centuries. And every one of them is reaching toward a home that's already changed—or that they've changed too much to simply walk back into.

00:05:15.361 --> 00:05:23.360
<v Lina>But distance does something stranger, too—it enlarges the ordinary. That's where we're headed next.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:31.680
<v Maya>So we've been talking about what it means to leave. But distance does this strange thing — it doesn't just separate you from a place.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.000
<v Theo>It takes every ordinary thing you left behind and makes it radiant.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:43.920
<v Lina>And what he's longing for isn't grand at all. It's voices. Songs. Faces.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:52.479
<v Maya>I sigh for rest and the sound of home
voices, the music of home songs, the light of loved eyes and
faces

00:05:52.479 --> 00:06:00.320
<v Theo>He pictures himself on a little island in the boundless ocean, surrounded by travelers gambling over champagne on a Sunday.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.280
<v Maya>The Sunday champagne really bothered him.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:11.919
<v Lina>He is also harsh about foreign faces and tongues in ways that are very much of his era. That's his period-bound lens, not ours.

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:22.960
<v Maya>But that impulse — cataloguing exactly what you miss — it doesn't go away. A blogger named Emily does the same thing in 2012, from Kenya.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:32.079
<v Theo>She's on a site called One Trailing Spouse, and she writes this list of five things she misses about the U.S. after five months abroad.

00:06:32.079 --> 00:06:35.839
<v Lina>And the list is so specific it's almost funny.

00:06:35.839 --> 00:06:38.319
<v Maya>How specific are we talking?

00:06:38.319 --> 00:06:43.627
<v Theo>Purple corduroy pants. Her muffin tin. Netflix.

00:06:43.627 --> 00:06:46.508
<v Maya>The muffin tin is the one that gets me.

00:06:46.508 --> 00:06:56.027
<v Lina>And driving — just the freedom to get in a car by herself after dark. She's candid that this is a privileged perspective, she says so.

00:06:56.027 --> 00:07:01.228
<v Maya>But missing the feeling of casual safety? That's real no matter how you frame it.

00:07:01.228 --> 00:07:12.534
<v Maya>A commenter named Marina chimes in from England and says she hasn't worn completely dry clothes in nearly two years. She misses the sun. And avocados.

00:07:12.534 --> 00:07:17.014
<v Theo>Honestly? Avocados can do that.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:29.040
<v Lina>Then there's Kathleen, on a WordPress blog called My 8×10 Life. She grew up in the rural Midwest, moved reluctantly to Brooklyn.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:39.440
<v Maya>And she writes about driving back through those Midwestern landscapes and feeling like she could finally stretch and breathe again — after months of swallowing the grit of New York.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:47.200
<v Maya>But she says something that really gets me. She talks about the details of home growing fuzzy, and it bothers her.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:53.279
<v Theo>Like the feelings of belonging are still sharp, but the specifics — they're slipping away.

00:07:53.279 --> 00:08:04.400
<v Lina>But, I don’t think I’ll ever allow myself to like it, and will never love it no matter how well I adapt to the urban life. I guess it makes me feel disloyal to my roots.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:14.800
<v Theo>Disloyal. That word. Homesickness isn't just missing a place — it becomes a kind of moral obligation to keep missing it.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:27.600
<v Maya>We found another exile — a memoir fragment from the Internet Archive's OCR pipeline. Someone marking a thirty-seventh birthday in May 1919, living in seclusion on an island.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:38.161
<v Theo>The narrator doesn't fully identify themselves in this fragment, but letters and flowers arrive from Germany — so many the small rooms of a parsonage can't hold them all.

00:08:38.161 --> 00:08:45.601
<v Lina>What struck me is how the narrator measures belonging — not by being present, but by being remembered.

00:08:45.601 --> 00:08:55.201
<v Maya>Those letters prove people still feel connected across distance and, as the writer puts it, across a wildly raging campaign of slander.

00:08:55.201 --> 00:09:05.281
<v Theo>And we should be honest — we don't know from this fragment alone who the narrator is or what the slander was about. The text starts mid-sentence.

00:09:05.281 --> 00:09:08.081
<v Lina>That's the nature of digitized scraps.

00:09:08.081 --> 00:09:10.880
<v Lina>A gap is not permission to invent.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:15.761
<v Maya>Which brings us to a passage that kind of names the whole feeling we've been circling.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:25.360
<v Theo>From what appears to be volume twenty-one of the collected works of someone abbreviated 'Stev' in the filename — an essay about roads.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:31.119
<v Lina>And the writer introduces this German word: Sehnsucht.

00:09:31.119 --> 00:09:36.000
<v Lina>The passion for what is ever beyond. That ache for the horizon.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.360
<v Theo>And the essay quotes Goethe's Werther to make a devastating point.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:50.752
<v Lina>When you finally reach the distance you wanted—when 'there' becomes 'here'—everything is afterwards as it was before.

00:09:50.752 --> 00:09:54.271
<v Maya>So arrival can make the dream smaller?

00:09:54.271 --> 00:09:58.911
<v Theo>Exactly. You arrive, and the magic collapses.

00:09:58.911 --> 00:10:05.312
<v Maya>Wheeler sighing for home voices. Kathleen gripping the steering wheel through flat fields.

00:10:05.312 --> 00:10:11.792
<v Lina>Emily dreaming of purple pants. The unnamed exile counting birthday flowers.

00:10:11.792 --> 00:10:17.473
<v Theo>They're all living inside that word: Sehnsucht.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:24.959
<v Maya>But Sehnsucht changes when the distant place becomes a destination. What happens when you actually go back?

00:10:24.959 --> 00:10:28.400
<v Theo>So we found a story in the crawl that lands right on this.

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:41.520
<v Lina>First-person essay by Christal Presley—originally published on CNN, then republished on a New Hampshire TV station's website, which is where the crawler actually caught it.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:46.721
<v Maya>She's driving from Atlanta back to Honaker, Virginia. Deep Appalachia.

00:10:46.721 --> 00:10:51.921
<v Lina>And she hasn't really been back in almost twenty years. Left at eighteen.

00:10:51.921 --> 00:11:00.721
<v Maya>And she draws this parallel that just—she says it plainly: that was the same age her father was when he was drafted and sent to Vietnam.

00:11:00.721 --> 00:11:13.121
<v Maya>I'm going home to tell my truth. I was 18 when I fled there -- the same age my father was when he was drafted and sent to Vietnam. We both ended up scarred by his war.

00:11:13.121 --> 00:11:19.360
<v Theo>Both scarred by his war. That line just sits there.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:25.521
<v Lina>Her father came home with PTSD, and the family kept it secret for years.

00:11:25.521 --> 00:11:31.361
<v Maya>She describes the secret growing inside her like a cancer—those are her words.

00:11:31.361 --> 00:11:38.321
<v Maya>And that's what shaped how she saw home. She writes that she'd grown to hate the mountains—the whole town.

00:11:38.321 --> 00:11:43.920
<v Theo>Because she associated all of it with the secrets she was never able to tell.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:51.281
<v Theo>Now here's where the archive does something frustrating and weirdly poetic. The captured text cuts off mid-sentence.

00:11:51.281 --> 00:12:03.601
<v Lina>She's arrived at her parents' house, there's a clash with her father over a song he wrote, and—it just stops. Mid-word. We literally don't get the resolution.

00:12:03.601 --> 00:12:10.480
<v Maya>So the crawl preserved the wound but not the healing. If there even was one in that moment.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:21.441
<v Theo>And while Christal's return is about confronting trauma—we found another piece from barely thirty miles away, same region of Virginia, that's about a much quieter kind of loss.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:37.439
<v Lina>A woman named Melva posted a poem on a personal website called Heavens Gates, describing her grandparents' homeplace in Smith Hollow near Abingdon.

00:12:37.439 --> 00:12:45.120
<v Maya>The house is physically falling apart. Broken windows, rotting walls, vines climbing through the cracks.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:54.160
<v Theo>But Melva layers in all these memories—a little girl hunting for hen's nests, riding a bike down the hill, her grandmother calling her in for supper.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:58.722
<v Lina>With love and laughter inside those rotting walls

00:12:58.722 --> 00:13:02.403
<v Theo>And the page itself is kind of a rotting house.

00:13:02.403 --> 00:13:04.563
<v Maya>A MIDI file?

00:13:04.563 --> 00:13:09.843
<v Theo>Set to auto-play—a song called "Old Home Place."

00:13:09.843 --> 00:13:15.522
<v Lina>There's also a boilerplate lyrics disclaimer that doesn't apply to her original poem.

00:13:15.522 --> 00:13:23.844
<v Maya>The poem is dated 2009, but the memorial-site design feels older. The crawler found it in 2013.

00:13:23.844 --> 00:13:27.043
<v Theo>The old web had confidence.

00:13:27.043 --> 00:13:34.083
<v Lina>And this pattern—going back and being stunned by the change—it's not new at all.

00:13:34.083 --> 00:13:44.483
<v Maya>We found a speech from a scanned book on Archive.org, someone addressing the town of Holland, Massachusetts. His family farmed there four generations.

00:13:44.483 --> 00:13:54.163
<v Theo>He left as a teenager, fought in the Civil War with the First Michigan Sharpshooters, and was—in his words—dreadfully homesick.

00:13:54.163 --> 00:14:00.322
<v Maya>Then there's another scanned speech—someone returning to America after twenty years abroad.

00:14:00.322 --> 00:14:06.722
<v Theo>He compares himself to Rip Van Winkle, almost appalled at the wonderful changes that meet his eyes.

00:14:06.722 --> 00:14:14.323
<v Lina>He's been invited to dinners everywhere, but he only says yes to his native town of Danvers, Massachusetts.

00:14:14.323 --> 00:14:17.603
<v Lina>Homecoming as time travel.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:25.681
<v Maya>So we've talked about leaving and coming back. But here's what nobody warns you about.

00:14:25.681 --> 00:14:34.080
<v Lina>Sometimes the place is still on the map, still has a zip code, and it is just… not yours anymore.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:47.321
<v Theo>And we've got two writers in these archives doing almost the exact same thing—both walking through their hometowns, taking inventory. One's in Mt. Union, Iowa, small-town Henry County.

00:14:47.321 --> 00:14:56.240
<v Lina>The other's on a courthouse square in his little Texas town. Both published around May 2013, both crawled that December.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:00.801
<v Maya>One from the Ottumwa Courier, one from a Houston Chronicle blog.

00:15:00.801 --> 00:15:13.281
<v Lina>The Iowa writer brings their daughter and baby grandson Max along. Three generations standing in a cemetery, leaving flowers at family graves. And there's this line—

00:15:13.281 --> 00:15:19.680
<v Lina>Tears fell in the cemetery where we showed Max the resting places of his great-grandparents.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:35.920
<v Lina>It gave me pause when I finally realized that I love the memory of that part of Henry County. That even though the town is still there, many of the places I knew so well are gone. And most of the people I knew who lived there are gone, too.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:45.681
<v Theo>We should say—that column is truncated. It ends mid-sentence: 'I walked past stones,' and then nothing.

00:15:45.681 --> 00:15:57.521
<v Lina>The crawler grabbed a printer-friendly page variant, you can see '/print' in the URL, and whether the full text ever made it into the archive… we just don't know.

00:15:57.521 --> 00:16:08.080
<v Maya>The Texas writer has the same mid-sentence cutoff! He's walking the courthouse square, remembering every storefront, and then—gone.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:21.601
<v Theo>And his text opens with the string 'Print67' smashed right into the first paragraph—a share-counter widget the crawler swallowed as regular text. Classic WET extraction artifact.

00:16:21.601 --> 00:16:23.921
<v Maya>Print sixty-seven?

00:16:23.921 --> 00:16:26.882
<v Theo>The machine thought the counter was prose.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:37.360
<v Maya>But the memories themselves—so specific. He worked as a soda skeet for seventeen and a half cents an hour.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:39.119
<v Lina>A soda skeet?

00:16:39.119 --> 00:16:48.960
<v Lina>A bowl of chili for a dime—and then you just kept adding crackers and ketchup until you felt full. That's not nostalgia as mood.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:51.120
<v Maya>That's nostalgia as exact price.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:55.359
<v Theo>A dime, plus structural crackers.

00:16:55.359 --> 00:17:02.159
<v Theo>He remembers carrying groceries from the Piggly Wiggly two or three blocks to somebody's house, putting the milk in their ice box.

00:17:02.159 --> 00:17:05.999
<v Lina>A whole economy of proximity that's just… vanished.

00:17:05.999 --> 00:17:08.560
<v Maya>And an actual ice box.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:15.200
<v Maya>Now, both of those writers are wistful. But the Andover letter? That's a different temperature entirely.

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:22.640
<v Theo>This is a letter to the editor of the Andover Townsman, published November 2012, crawled over a year later.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:29.680
<v Lina>A man comes back to Andover, Massachusetts after decades away and he is angry.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:37.440
<v Maya>The town used to be known as 'Mill, Hill and Till'—and now he calls it a smaller version of New York City.

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:50.400
<v Lina>He goes through landmarks one by one—the Shawsheen River, historic dams, the old high school—and each one is either demolished, degraded, or renamed.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:54.480
<v Maya>He sees the river and compares it to a Love Canal wasteland.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:18:03.041
<v Maya>There's something uncomfortable in it too, though. Part of his argument is that newcomers don't understand the town's history, that they've diluted its values.

00:18:03.041 --> 00:18:11.760
<v Theo>And that raises a real question—who gets to define a place's identity? The person who left, or the people who live there now?

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:14.481
<v Theo>The person who stayed might answer very differently.

00:18:14.481 --> 00:18:25.840
<v Lina>That's the tension. The Iowa and Texas writers grieve what's gone but hold it lightly—they know the loss is partly theirs, inside them.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:34.241
<v Maya>The Andover letter says no, the town itself is wrong now. And I think that's a really human fork in the road when your hometown changes.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:50.480
<v Theo>Meanwhile there's a very different kind of homecoming buried in a personal blog post from Christmas Eve, 2009—captured nearly four years later.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:19:02.720
<v Lina>Someone's home visiting family, loves them genuinely, but feels this ache because London has become their real center of gravity. They can't quite name the feeling.

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:15.040
<v Lina>But the fragment is enough. It's someone realizing that home has split in two—the place where your family is, and the place where you became yourself.

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:17.360
<v Maya>And those might never be the same place again.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:27.200
<v Maya>And then there's a songwriter named Xanthe Littlemore who puts that exact feeling to music—or at least a lyrics site tried to.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:36.480
<v Theo>The crawler grabbed preview snippets from a page whose URL slug says 'hurry up with the robots,' which doesn't match any song title on the page.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:48.160
<v Theo>Total metadata mismatch. And the lyrics themselves are gated—only preview fragments got through. There's even a Unicode replacement character where the encoding broke.

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:50.961
<v Lina>We are literally reading through frosted glass.

00:19:50.961 --> 00:19:54.001
<v Maya>Frosted glass is exactly it.

00:19:54.001 --> 00:19:59.521
<v Lina>But look what survived. Under the title 'Don't Say Goodbye'—

00:19:59.521 --> 00:20:14.320
<v Lina>There is a town where I used to live as a child
They tore down everything that was growing wild
And I don't like going back home, I hate to see what they've done
'Cause when I do, well, it makes me cry

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:19.840
<v Theo>We can't even hear the melody. The full lyrics were behind a login wall.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:26.962
<v Lina>A few lines from a song by an artist most people haven't heard of, on a page that doesn't match its own URL.

00:20:26.962 --> 00:20:31.841
<v Maya>That's the archive—generous and stingy in the same breath.

00:20:31.841 --> 00:20:38.161
<v Lina>What strikes me across all of these is that no one finds what they went back for.

00:20:38.161 --> 00:20:48.322
<v Maya>Not the cemetery visitor, not the courthouse walker, not the angry letter writer, not the blogger at Christmas. The place can't hold what they need it to hold.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:54.800
<v Theo>So what do you do with the love that has nowhere to land?

00:20:54.800 --> 00:21:05.120
<v Maya>Sometimes the place can't hold the love, so a smell, a logo, or an old object does. That's where these stories take us next.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:08.960
<v Lina>And sometimes the longing isn't even for something you actually had.

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:19.439
<v Maya>Okay, this one genuinely surprised me. There's a blogger who ran a site called Branded in the 80s — basically a personal archive of childhood consumer culture.

00:21:19.439 --> 00:21:24.880
<v Theo>And he's writing about how old packaging, old toys, can hit you like a physical force.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:29.119
<v Theo>He's not being poetic about it, either. He means it literally.

00:21:29.119 --> 00:21:36.800
<v Maya>There have been moments where I stumble across something that I completely forgot about and literally doubled over as if I were punched in the gut.

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:49.040
<v Lina>His example is painfully specific: an Otter Pop, a hot green electrical transformer box, surf shorts, and a friend talking skateboarding.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:57.200
<v Theo>He can still feel that box burning through the fabric. That's not a concept. That's a full-body memory.

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:07.520
<v Maya>But then he finds a People magazine ad for Lacoste polo shirts — the little alligator patch — and he gets that same gut-punch of nostalgia.

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.560
<v Theo>And then he realizes: he never owned one.

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:13.920
<v Lina>Wait—nostalgia for a shirt he never owned?

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:18.960
<v Theo>He calls it false nostalgia — longing for something you never actually had.

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:28.800
<v Lina>He grew up in Florida surrounded by gator imagery, so the Lacoste logo felt like home even though the brand was never in his closet.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:35.759
<v Maya>It played into this sense that I had at the time that Florida was the center of the country, the center of cool

00:22:35.759 --> 00:22:43.126
<v Theo>Now — web-artifact note — we're actually reading this from two different tag-archive pages on his blog.

00:22:43.126 --> 00:22:50.807
<v Lina>One filed under 'Lacoste Gator,' one under 'False Nostalgia.' Same post, two doorways.

00:22:50.807 --> 00:22:59.207
<v Maya>And both versions cut off mid-sentence with a bare less-than character — the crawler hit an image tag it couldn't extract.

00:22:59.207 --> 00:23:05.207
<v Theo>He was probably showing us the little alligator logo, but that image is just… gone.

00:23:05.207 --> 00:23:08.647
<v Maya>The missing image is the thing doing all the work.

00:23:08.647 --> 00:23:12.647
<v Lina>That same ache turns up across centuries.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:18.640
<v Maya>Right beside this blog post in the crawl is a letter from the eighteen hundreds.

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:27.040
<v Theo>Its writer is explaining to a reverend named Cochrane why he cannot attend the centennial of a town called Antrim.

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:36.239
<v Maya>And for this man, Antrim isn't just a town. It's the only place on earth he could call home. For thirty-eight years.

00:23:36.239 --> 00:23:42.799
<v Theo>For thirty-eight years it has been the only spot on earth that I could call by that peerless name, — home!

00:23:42.799 --> 00:23:53.440
<v Lina>He buried his father, his mother, three sisters, and a brother in that town's cemeteries. His only son was born there. He entered the ministry there.

00:23:53.440 --> 00:24:04.161
<v Maya>The homestead at Clinton holds — as he puts it — all those memories, painful and joyous, that give the word home its indescribable fascination.

00:24:04.161 --> 00:24:14.560
<v Theo>And we only have this letter because it was printed in a commemorative book, scanned and OCR'd into a plain-text stream on the Internet Archive.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:22.721
<v Lina>There's a stray page header jammed into the middle of it — 'Response of Col. Silas Dinsmore' — which isn't him at all.

00:24:22.721 --> 00:24:25.521
<v Maya>Just the scanner turning a page.

00:24:25.521 --> 00:24:29.761
<v Lina>A page header just walked into somebody else's letter.

00:24:29.761 --> 00:24:35.601
<v Maya>A Hancock County history gives us another voice from a similar commemorative book.

00:24:35.601 --> 00:24:44.241
<v Theo>Reverend William Nichols stands at an old settlers' meeting and says something that could be an instruction manual for this podcast.

00:24:44.241 --> 00:24:52.321
<v Theo>At old settlers' meetings it is fitting and proper to call up the past and rehearse memories and events past and gone.

00:24:52.321 --> 00:24:56.721
<v Lina>Nichols was born before Indiana was even a state.

00:24:56.721 --> 00:25:04.081
<v Theo>He remembers hiding in a hollow stump at age four because a party of Indigenous people came to his father's house to trade.

00:25:04.081 --> 00:25:09.281
<v Maya>We need to mark the frame: this is a settler's account of that encounter.

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:20.799
<v Lina>His childhood fear was real to him, but the story does not include the perspective of the people who were already there.

00:25:20.799 --> 00:25:24.959
<v Maya>And that missing perspective matters as much as the remembered fear.

00:25:24.959 --> 00:25:32.479
<v Theo>And his schoolhouse — a log hut with windows made by cutting out a log and pasting oiled paper over the hole.

00:25:32.479 --> 00:25:36.080
<v Lina>That's the landscape these people are building memory from.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:42.320
<v Maya>Which brings us to someone who grew up in a landscape most people wouldn't think to feel nostalgic about at all.

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:53.519
<v Theo>A professor named Michael Rustad, writing about the Red River Valley in northwestern Minnesota — flat black-loam prairie, endless horizon.

00:25:53.519 --> 00:25:56.080
<v Lina>I grew up longing to leave the flat land.

00:25:56.080 --> 00:26:10.159
<v Lina>And what changed him was a sixth-grade teacher — Dale Finney Miller — who taught Scandinavian mythology and local history, the Alexandria runestone, stories about the land under his feet.

00:26:10.159 --> 00:26:13.920
<v Maya>That teacher made the place legible to him. Made it worth loving.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:27.920
<v Maya>This essay was written in 2001, posted on a RootsWeb genealogy page — hand-coded HTML, no content-management system — and crawled twelve years later in December 2013.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:31.359
<v Theo>We have no idea if anyone touched it in between.

00:26:31.359 --> 00:26:42.280
<v Theo>The extraction cuts off mid-sentence too. He was about to say something about learning from his teachers, and then — nothing. The crawler just stopped.

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:44.319
<v Lina>Again?

00:26:44.319 --> 00:26:53.760
<v Theo>Every one of these people — the blogger, the minister, Nichols, Rustad — the archive caught them.

00:26:53.760 --> 00:27:02.160
<v Lina>But it can't give us back the alligator logo, or the minister's full name, or the end of Rustad's sentence.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:06.560
<v Maya>It preserves the ache without restoring the thing that was ached for.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:15.520
<v Maya>So we've been sitting with all these places people leave behind, and the things they build to hold onto them.

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:24.240
<v Theo>I want to end with a guy who did something I find kind of beautiful. He named his whole company after a park.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:29.279
<v Theo>Ed Romanoff. He founded a company called PineRock.

00:27:29.279 --> 00:27:44.560
<v Lina>And his about-us page—crawled December 2013—has this origin story: when he was a kid in Connecticut, there was a park called PineRock Park. Baseball, live music, giant swings.

00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:47.760
<v Maya>And he just… kept carrying it.

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:50.719
<v Lina>He named the future after the feeling.

00:27:50.719 --> 00:28:01.839
<v Maya>I didn't know what I wanted to be when I grew up, but I did know that I wanted to create something that moved me in that same way. And I decided that when I did, I would call it PineRock.

00:28:01.839 --> 00:28:08.560
<v Lina>Okay, yes, it's a corporate page—it's branding. But the impulse underneath it is real.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:14.320
<v Maya>You take a childhood feeling and pour it into a name so it outlives the place itself.

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:24.961
<v Maya>And that's what we keep finding. The archive holds evidence of longing, not the thing longed for. And sometimes the longing shows up in the weirdest containers.

00:28:24.961 --> 00:28:28.880
<v Theo>Theo, tell them about the Halloween candle guy.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:41.599
<v Theo>Okay. So there's a blogger on Branded in the 80s—writes about retro toys, ThunderCats, G.I. Joe, the whole universe. And he's self-aware about it. He actually says—

00:28:41.599 --> 00:28:49.360
<v Theo>“This junk doesn’t hold a candle to the stuff from back in my day…” I’m not that guy, or at least I don’t want to be.

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:52.879
<v Lina>He calls himself an amateur semiotician.

00:28:52.879 --> 00:28:55.520
<v Maya>On a blog about Smurfs.

00:28:55.520 --> 00:28:58.400
<v Theo>Which is a wonderful sentence.

00:28:58.400 --> 00:29:07.360
<v Maya>But then he says his year has been rough. And Halloween is the one season where he can let go and just feel something good.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:19.680
<v Theo>He ends up at a Yankee Candle browsing the Boney Bunch collection with his spouse, and the blog entry literally cuts off mid-sentence during that visit.

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:23.439
<v Theo>And there goes the sentence again.

00:29:23.439 --> 00:29:31.199
<v Maya>Fragments keep being the theme. There's a poem on PoetrySoup by Andrew Nashat called "Do you remember."

00:29:31.199 --> 00:29:36.719
<v Lina>We only have a name on a community poetry site; we don't know more about the writer.

00:29:36.719 --> 00:29:41.680
<v Theo>The whole poem is built on that phrase, repeated like a heartbeat.

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:57.520
<v Lina>I remember the days when all we wanted to do was be together, I remember the days when all we wanted to do was be there for each other, I remember the days when all we did was reminisce our childhood memories, I remember those days my dear.

00:29:57.520 --> 00:30:01.840
<v Theo>That repetition sounds like someone trying to keep a door open.

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:12.080
<v Maya>And then it shifts. He asks: why is there a block of ice forming between us? Home isn't a place in this poem. It's a person.

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:15.199
<v Theo>And that person is slipping away.

00:30:15.199 --> 00:30:19.359
<v Theo>Which brings us—weirdly—all the way back to Beethoven.

00:30:19.359 --> 00:30:26.400
<v Lina>The crawl picked up an OCR scan of a printed edition of his letters, served as a text file on archive.org.

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:35.439
<v Maya>And in this fragment—and I mean fragment, it opens and closes mid-sentence—he's writing about leaving his fatherland and about a friend.

00:30:35.439 --> 00:30:41.199
<v Lina>since I left my fatherland, you are one of those to whom my heart specially clings.

00:30:41.199 --> 00:30:47.841
<v Maya>He's talking about Stephan von Breuning, a childhood friend who has come back into his life.

00:30:47.841 --> 00:30:52.239
<v Lina>Then he says he has to withdraw from everyone because of his hearing loss.

00:30:52.239 --> 00:30:57.599
<v Theo>The people he most wants to reach are the very people he is being pulled away from.

00:30:57.599 --> 00:31:05.120
<v Lina>Think about the layers between us and that voice. A hand writes a letter. An editor prints it.

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:08.320
<v Maya>A scanner reads it. A crawler captures it.

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:21.040
<v Theo>Then we find it two hundred years later in a file called beethovensletter001beet underscore djvu dot txt.

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:24.240
<v Maya>That filename is practically sediment.

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:34.800
<v Lina>And that's what an archive actually does, isn't it. A company named for a park that may be gone. A Halloween blog that stops mid-sentence.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:43.680
<v Maya>A poem to someone drifting away. A letter from a man going deaf, reaching for a friend. The archive keeps the reaching.

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:46.959
<v Theo>It can't give back the thing that was reached for.

00:31:46.959 --> 00:31:58.160
<v Theo>We can't restore the home, the person, the page—or even the full context that produced any of these. We just know someone was here.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:01.840
<v Lina>And they wanted something badly enough to write it down.

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:07.600
<v Maya>And that the web remembered it—even partially, even broken—that matters.

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:22.642
<v Lina>Thanks for listening. The homes we leave behind don't disappear. They just change shape—into names, into rituals, into poems, into letters that open and close mid-sentence.

00:32:22.642 --> 00:32:24.160
<v Maya>We'll see you next time.
